\section{Read peaks, not the peptide roll-up}{


\code{peptides} is FlashLFQ's roll-up and \strong{it drops most match-between-runs transfers.} On
mzLib's own K562 pair there are \strong{140} true transfers in \code{peaks} and the peptide table shows
\strong{52} - a 63\% under-count. Worse, it is not evenly spread: per run the peaks give run_3
\strong{62} and run_4 \strong{78}, while the roll-up gives run_3 \strong{0} and run_4 52. Read only the
roll-up and MBR appears not to have worked at all in half the experiment.

"Peptides quantified in both runs" is \strong{257} from \code{peaks} and \strong{169} from \code{peptides}.

Nor can you reproduce the roll-up by pivoting \code{peaks} yourself: where a run has several peaks
for one peptide the roll-up reports one rather than their sum.

}
\section{\code{proteins$file_name} is a sample, not a file}{


FlashLFQ measures peptides in \strong{files} but resolves proteins across \strong{samples}, grouping runs
by condition and biological replicate before the median-polish roll-up. \code{flashlfq_quantify()}
gives every run its own sample, so \code{proteins$file_name} and \code{peptides$file_name} carry the same
run base names and the two frames join cleanly on it.

The distinction only bites if runs are ever grouped into replicates: \code{proteins$file_name} would
then hold a sample label (\code{"condition_replicate"}) covering several runs, while
\code{peptides$file_name} stayed per run. The column name is kept for symmetry between the two
frames; read it as "the thing this intensity was measured over".

}
\section{What 0 and NA mean, and which is rare}{


\code{peptides$intensity} is \strong{0} when the peptide was not measured in that run. It is never NA.

\code{proteins$intensity} is \strong{NA} when FlashLFQ could not resolve a number at all - its
median-polish produced NaN. Arithmetic propagates it, so \code{mean()} on a protein column returns
NA rather than a confidently wrong number, and \code{na.rm = TRUE} is a choice you make visibly.
mzLibR never applies it on your behalf.

\strong{NA is the rare outcome and 0 is the common one.} On the K562 pair, \strong{2} proteins are NA
and \strong{847} are 0 in both runs. "No usable number" is 849; "could not be resolved" is 2.

}
